          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following fetal membranes is responsible for producing the earliest</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following fetal membranes is responsible for producing the earliest blood cells in the embryo?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chorion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The chorion forms from the trophoblast and extraembryonic mesoderm. It contributes to the placenta and secretes hormones (hCG and hPL), but it plays no role in haematopoiesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Yolk sac</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The yolk sac is the site of earliest haematopoiesis in the embryo. It also gives rise to primordial germ cells before it eventually regresses.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Amnion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The amnion develops from the epiblast and forms the fluid-filled sac that cushions the fetus, regulates temperature, and permits free fetal movement. It has no haematopoietic function.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Allantois</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The allantois contributes to early blood vessel formation in the cord and later becomes the urachus connecting the bladder to the umbilicus. It does not produce blood cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember that each fetal membrane has a distinct role: the chorion makes hormones, the amnion cushions, the allantois forms vessels (and becomes the urachus), and the yolk sac produces the earliest blood cells and primordial germ cells."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The yolk sac is the earliest site of blood cell formation and also gives rise to primordial germ cells — two functions that distinguish it from the other fetal membranes."
                }
              ],
