          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What structural feature distinguishes tertiary chorionic villi from secondary vi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What structural feature distinguishes tertiary chorionic villi from secondary villi?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Presence of a trophoblast covering over the villus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>All stages of chorionic villi — primary, secondary, and tertiary — possess a trophoblast covering. This feature does not distinguish tertiary from secondary villi.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Entry of extraembryonic mesoderm into the villus core</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mesodermal invasion of the villus core is what distinguishes secondary villi from primary villi. By the tertiary stage, mesoderm is already present.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Formation of blood vessels within the mesodermal core</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Tertiary villi are defined by vascularisation — blood vessels form within the mesodermal core, making the villi functional for feto-maternal exchange.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Division of the villous tree into cotyledons by decidual septa</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cotyledons are macroscopic divisions of the term placenta created by septa from the decidua basalis. They are a gross anatomical feature, not a property of individual villi.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The three villus stages build on each other: primary (trophoblast only) → secondary (mesoderm enters the core) → tertiary (blood vessels form within the mesoderm). Vascularisation is the hallmark of tertiary villi."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Tertiary villi develop from the third week onward when blood vessels form inside the mesodermal core, making the villi truly vascularised and capable of nutrient and gas exchange."
                }
              ],
